## Exported and startup managed calls in msil-ld (ImplementationPlan.md
## 5.20.4 MR2 and MR4 as amended for dllexport, constructor and destructor
## on a managed-call declaration, milestone MC-1). clang defines one
## forwarder for each of these attributes
## (clang/test/CodeGen/MSIL/msil-method-forwarders.c), the backend compiles
## it like any function (llvm/test/CodeGen/MSIL/managed-call-forwarders.ll),
## and it calls the unit's stub '__msil_mc.<s>'; the 8th operand of the
## declaration's !msil.managed.calls entry lists the attributes. The members
## are FakeWidgets.il's (see managed-calls.test):
## - dllexport (Windows triples): the forwarder is '<s>' itself, weak_odr
##   dllexport in the COMDAT group '<s>'. Two units that export one managed
##   call (exp1.ll and exp2.ll: wguard, Contoso.Widget::Guarded(int32)) give
##   a -shared link whose export class has 'wguard' once, which calls the
##   stub, as CIL objects and as LTO inputs. A dllexport definition selects
##   the dllexport-only export mode on a Windows triple (5.9.3), so plain1
##   and plain2, GLOBAL functions without dllexport, are not exported. A
##   unit that declares the C name without the attribute (pmain.ll) binds
##   to the forwarder, in both input orders and as LTO inputs, so its call
##   reaches the stub (MR2's reason 4 for an exported name).
## - constructor: the forwarder is '__msil_mc_ctor.<s>', linkonce_odr hidden
##   in its own COMDAT group, and its llvm.global_ctors entry is keyed to
##   that group. Two units that declare one constructor managed call
##   (ctor1.ll and ctor2.ll: wreset, Contoso.Widget::Reset()) give a module
##   initializer that calls '__msil_mc_ctor.wreset' once, in object and LTO
##   links, on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil
##   and msil64-unknown-dotnet. On a 'static' declaration (sctor1.ll and
##   sctor2.ll: wsreset, whose stub is '__msil_mc.wsreset.__uniq.444' in
##   both units) the forwarder is internal and its entry has no key, so the
##   initializer calls each unit's forwarder: two calls.
## - T-M17's second form,
##   'managed call '<f>' is declared with different 'dllexport', 'constructor' or 'destructor' attributes in different translation units',
##   for two units whose entries differ only in those attributes: wreset as
##   constructor(65535) (ctor1.ll) and as constructor(101) (prio.ll), on
##   x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil, and wguard with and without dllexport
##   (exp1.ll and noexp.ll), on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil. msil-ld
##   decides T-M17 for each managed call that two units still declare, in
##   every link mode (5.20.4 MR4 and 5.20.3 T-M17 as amended in section 12).
##   In the LTO image and under --lto-emit-asm, with and without
##   --reference, it compares the bitcode inputs' entries before LTO, which
##   then does not run (slot P0 compared the merged module's entries before
##   that; these links used to give T-M17's first form). In object links it
##   compares the units' stubs, which agree here, then the forwarders that
##   the units share (the weak '<s>' in the COMDAT group '<s>', and the
##   priority of the 'Init' entry of '__msil_mc_ctor.<s>'), in both input
##   orders; these links used to keep the first unit's export or constructor
##   priority silently. Links that mix a CIL object and a bitcode file give
##   the second form too (exp1 and noexp, prio and ctor1, in both orders),
##   and so does exp1.o with noexp.bc and exp2.bc, in two orders, once: the
##   line used to come from the mixed check and again from slot P0.
## - A joined 8th operand (MR4's attributes text, which clang's CodeGen and
##   msil-ld both write with cli::printManagedCallAttributes): join1.ll and
##   join2.ll export wjoin, Contoso.Widget::Reset(), with constructor(300),
##   so their entries end in 'dllexport,constructor(300)', and a CIL
##   object's forwarders (the weak 'wjoin' in the COMDAT group 'wjoin' and
##   the 'Init' entry of '__msil_mc_ctor.wjoin' at priority 300) spell the
##   same text. As CIL objects, as bitcode and mixed, in both orders, they
##   link (-shared) to one export 'wjoin' that calls the stub and a module
##   initializer that calls '__msil_mc_ctor.wjoin' once. joinx.ll exports
##   wjoin without constructor(300), so its entry ends in 'dllexport':
##   beside join1.ll it gives T-M17's second form in object, LTO and mixed
##   links and under --lto-emit-asm, in both orders.
## - T-M17's first form,
##   'managed call '<f>' is declared with different 'msil_method' or 'msil_catch' arguments in different translation units',
##   for two units that declare one managed call with different arguments,
##   here with shared forwarders: wguard exported with
##   Contoso.Widget::Guarded(int32) (exp1.ll) and with
##   Contoso.Point::Twice(int32) (twice.ll); guarded exported with
##   msil_catch("Contoso.WidgetException", 5) (cexp5.ll) and with code 6
##   (cexp6.ll); and wreset as a constructor of Contoso.Widget::Reset()
##   (ctor1.ll) and of Rewind() (rewind.ll), a member that FakeWidgets does
##   not have, so the line must stop the link before binding. In object
##   links msil-ld compares the units' stubs (-shared for the exports, once
##   without --reference; the constructor on x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil
##   and msil64-unknown-dotnet); in LTO links it compares the bitcode
##   inputs' entries before LTO (slot P0 compared the merged module's
##   entries before that); in links that mix a CIL object and a bitcode
##   file it compares the object's stub with the stub that code generation
##   makes of the bitcode's declaration: -shared with exp1 and twice and
##   with cexp5 and cexp6, an executable whose main calls the exported name
##   (pmain.o), and the constructor on x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil and
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet; and exp1.o with twice as a ThinLTO module and
##   with twice.bc under --lto-partitions=2. Each link (in both input
##   orders, except the partitioned one) reports the line once and writes no
##   output. The mixed links used to link silently with one unit's member
##   (or fail to bind Rewind). Mixed links of units that agree still link:
##   exp1 and exp2 (one export, whose stub calls Guarded) and ctor1 and
##   ctor2 (one constructor call, on x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil and
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet), in both orders. On msil64-unknown-dotnet the
##   stub that code generation makes of the bitcode's declaration has that
##   triple's data layout: the comparison neither skips it (ctor1 and
##   rewind) nor finds a difference that the units do not have (ctor1 and
##   ctor2).
## - ThinLTO and LTO partitions (the section 12 row '5.20.4 MR4 amended
##   (ThinLTO and LTO partitions)', and 5.20.4 MR4 and 5.20.3 T-M17 as
##   amended in section 12): no slot P0 sees the entries of exactly the
##   units that still declare a managed call. A ThinLTO backend (a module
##   made with opt -module-summary) sees another module's entries only with
##   a function it imports, every partition of --lto-partitions=2 sees them
##   all, and a merged module keeps the entry of a unit whose optimizer
##   deleted the declaration (managed-calls-units.test). So in every LTO
##   mode, full LTO with one module included, msil-ld compares the entries
##   of the bitcode inputs before LTO, by slot P0's rule (ASSEMBLY only
##   under --lto-emit-asm, managed-calls-assembly.test), reports each name
##   once, also when the mixed check above decides it too, and then does
##   not run LTO; when LTO runs, the modules that it compiles no longer
##   carry the list, so slot P0 compares no entries. First form: exp1 and
##   twice, and ctor1 and rewind, as ThinLTO modules in both orders; exp1 as
##   a ThinLTO module with twice.bc; exp1.bc and twice.bc under
##   --lto-partitions=2. Second form: exp1 and noexp as ThinLTO modules in
##   both orders; exp1.bc and noexp.bc under --lto-partitions=2; exp1.o with
##   noexp and exp2 as ThinLTO modules, in two orders, and with noexp.bc and
##   exp2.bc under --lto-partitions=2. The ThinLTO pairs used to link with
##   one unit's declaration (or fail to bind Rewind), and the partitioned
##   links to print the line once per partition (and once more from the
##   mixed check). Units without forwarders give the first form too: wpick
##   is Contoso.Widget::Guarded in pick1.ll and Contoso.Point::Twice in
##   pick2.ll and pick3.ll. ThinLTO would import pick1's pickA into pick2's
##   module, whose main calls it, and with it pick1's entry, but imports
##   nothing into pick3's, so pick1 and pick3 used to link with a stub of
##   each member (a full LTO link of the two gets the same line, which came
##   from slot P0 before msil-ld decided it).
##   With --save-temps such a link writes no output, no LTO object and no
##   bitcode file: four of them (exp1 with twice as ThinLTO modules, exp1.o
##   with twice as a ThinLTO module, and exp1.bc and exp1.o with twice.bc
##   under --lto-partitions=2) each write into a fresh directory, which also
##   holds their ThinLTO inputs because ThinLTO writes a module's
##   temporaries next to it, and the directory keeps only those inputs and,
##   possibly, '<output>.resolution.txt' (the test removes it before listing
##   the directory). Units that agree still link: exp1 and exp2 as ThinLTO
##   modules and under --lto-partitions=2 (one export, whose stub calls
##   Guarded), and ctor1 and ctor2 as ThinLTO modules (one constructor
##   call).
## - A bitcode input whose metadata does not read (the section 12 row
##   '5.20.7 MK6 amended'): damage.py writes exp2.bc with the 8 bytes before
##   its first '__msil_mc.wguard', the string lengths of the module's
##   METADATA_STRINGS record, set to 0xff, so opt fails with 'Unterminated
##   VBR' while the symbol table still reads. Beside exp1.o (both export
##   wguard, -shared) and beside noexp.o (the second form's pair), in both
##   orders, msil-ld's managed-call check reports
##   '<file>: Unterminated VBR' for exp2-bad.bc as the link's only error
##   line. It used to drop the input (a false second form beside exp1.o,
##   none beside noexp.o), and LTO then failed with 'Unterminated VBR'
##   without the file's name. Beside exp1.bc in full LTO, in both orders, the
##   line is the same, since msil-ld reads the bitcode inputs that declare a
##   managed call before LTO in every LTO mode (5.20.4 MR4 and 5.20.3 T-M17
##   as amended in section 12); that link used to fail in LTO with
##   'Unterminated VBR' without the file's name.
## - T-M21,
##   'managed call '<f>' is exported from <A>, but <B> also defines '<f>'':
##   any other non-local definition of an exported managed call's C name
##   would replace the weak forwarder silently: a strong one (cdef.ll)
##   always wins symbol resolution (5.9.5), and a weak one outside the
##   COMDAT group (cweak.ll: __attribute__((weak))) wins when it comes
##   first. So each is refused, whatever the input order and whichever
##   definition resolution keeps, <A> being the first input in load order
##   that has the forwarder (exp1, or exp2 before it) and <B> the other
##   definition's, in one line per name: in object, LTO and mixed links (a
##   CIL object with a bitcode file, either way), in both input orders, for
##   -shared outputs and for executables whose main calls the exported name
##   (pmain.ll). The weak definition used to link silently, and its
##   body replaced the export whenever it came first. An archive member is
##   extracted only for an undefined name: pmain.o libb.a exp1.o extracts
##   libb.a's cweak.o for pmain.o's call and gives T-M21 naming
##   'libb.a(cweak.o)' (libb-bc.a's cweak.bc likewise, under LTO), while
##   pmain.o exp1.o libb.a extracts nothing and links with no diagnostic:
##   the export 'wguard' calls the stub, and no 'wguard' body of cweak.ll
##   reaches the image.
## - destructor: the forwarder '__msil_mc_dtor.<s>' meets the error that
##   every global destructor gets until destructors work (M29 on the host
##   triples, P-2's LowerGlobalDtors on the portable one), naming the C
##   function:
##   'global destructor @<s> is not supported for MSIL yet' (dtor.ll: wfini),
##   from llc -filetype=obj, in LTO image links and under --lto-emit-asm, on
##   the three triples. It used to name the forwarder.
## - T-M23's forwarder form,
##   '<file>: defines '<name>', which is reserved for the MSIL toolchain (a managed-call forwarder)':
##   '__msil_mc_ctor.<s>' and '__msil_mc_dtor.<s>' are reserved like the
##   stubs' '__msil_mc.<s>' (T-M23's stub form: managed-calls-checks.test,
##   managed-calls-lto.test), and only the forwarder that clang makes may
##   define them: a weak hidden definition in a COMDAT group of its own name,
##   in an input that declares the managed call <s>, with, in a CIL object,
##   an init entry (constructor) or a fini entry (destructor) for it. gctor.ll
##   and gdtor.ll define '__msil_mc_ctor.wreset' and '__msil_mc_dtor.wreset'
##   as GLOBAL functions (hand-written IR: in C only an asm label could name
##   them, and clang refuses it with T-M22's forwarder form) beside
##   ctor1.ll's constructor managed call wreset. In object, LTO and mixed
##   links, in both input orders, on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil,
##   x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil and msil64-unknown-dotnet, msil-ld names
##   the input that defines the name and writes no output. These links used
##   to succeed silently, the definition replacing or dropping the
##   constructor managed call. The forwarders that clang makes are never
##   reported: the links above that succeed have no diagnostic, and dtor.ll's
##   links have only the destructor error.

# REQUIRES: llvm-cil-dump
# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: mkdir refs o lto
# RUN: yaml2obj %S/Inputs/fakepack/FakeRuntime.yaml -o refs/FakeRuntime.dll
# RUN: yaml2obj %S/Inputs/fakepack/FakeWidgets.yaml -o refs/FakeWidgets.dll
# DEFINE: %{crash} = --implicit-check-not="PLEASE submit" --implicit-check-not="Stack dump" \
# DEFINE:   --implicit-check-not="LLVM ERROR"
# DEFINE: %{err} = FileCheck %s %{crash} --implicit-check-not=error: --implicit-check-not=warning:
# DEFINE: %{listing} = %python %S/Inputs/shared/listing.py
# DEFINE: %{tri} =

## dllexport: one export from two units; only the dllexport definitions.
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj exp1.ll -o exp1.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj exp2.ll -o exp2.o
# RUN: llvm-as exp1.ll -o exp1.bc
# RUN: llvm-as exp2.ll -o exp2.bc
# RUN: msil-ld -shared exp1.o exp2.o --reference=refs -o o/exp.dll 2>&1 | count 0
# RUN: llvm-cil-dump o/exp.dll > exp-o.txt
# RUN: %{listing} members exp-o.txt exp | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT
# RUN: %{listing} method exp-o.txt exp wguard | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT-BODY
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT-STUB --input-file=exp-o.txt
# RUN: msil-ld -shared exp1.bc exp2.bc --reference=refs -o lto/exp.dll 2>&1 | count 0
# RUN: llvm-cil-dump lto/exp.dll > exp-lto.txt
# RUN: %{listing} members exp-lto.txt exp | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT
# RUN: %{listing} method exp-lto.txt exp wguard | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT-BODY
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT-STUB --input-file=exp-lto.txt
# EXPORT:           {{^}}.class 'exp' flags=0x00000181 extends [FakeRuntime]System.Object{{$}}
# EXPORT-NEXT:      {{^}}  .method '<msil>addr_wguard' native int() flags=0x0096 impl=0x0000{{$}}
# EXPORT-NEXT:      {{^}}  .method 'wguard' int32(int32) flags=0x0016 impl=0x0000{{$}}
# EXPORT-NEXT:      {{^}}    .param 1 'A_0' flags=0x0000{{$}}
# EXPORT-NOT:       {{.}}
# EXPORT-BODY:      {{^    L[0-9]+: }}ldarg.0{{$}}
# EXPORT-BODY-NEXT: {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call int32(int32) '<Module>'::'__msil_mc.wguard'{{$}}
# EXPORT-BODY-NEXT: {{^    L[0-9]+: }}ret{{$}}
# EXPORT-STUB:      {{^  }}.method '__msil_mc.wguard' int32(int32) flags=0x0013 impl=0x0000{{$}}
# EXPORT-STUB:      {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call int32(int32) [FakeWidgets]Contoso.Widget::'Guarded'{{$}}

## A plain declaration of the exported name binds to the forwarder; under
## LTO the forwarder may be inlined into main.
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj pmain.ll -o pmain.o
# RUN: llvm-as pmain.ll -o pmain.bc
# RUN: msil-ld pmain.o exp1.o --reference=refs -o o/pmain.exe 2>&1 | count 0
# RUN: msil-ld exp1.o pmain.o --reference=refs -o o/pmain2.exe 2>&1 | count 0
# RUN: llvm-cil-dump o/pmain.exe > pmain-o.txt
# RUN: %{listing} method pmain-o.txt '<Module>' main | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PMAIN
# RUN: %{listing} method pmain-o.txt '<Module>' wguard | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT-BODY
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT-STUB --input-file=pmain-o.txt
# RUN: msil-ld pmain.bc exp1.bc --reference=refs -o lto/pmain.exe 2>&1 | count 0
# RUN: llvm-cil-dump lto/pmain.exe > pmain-lto.txt
# RUN: %{listing} method pmain-lto.txt '<Module>' main | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PMAIN-LTO
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXPORT-STUB --input-file=pmain-lto.txt
# PMAIN:     {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call int32(int32) '<Module>'::'wguard'{{$}}
# PMAIN-LTO: {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call int32(int32) '<Module>'::'{{wguard|__msil_mc\.wguard}}'{{$}}

## constructor: once per program; once per unit for 'static'.
# DEFINE: %{ctor} = cat %{tri}.head ctor1.ll > ctor1-%{tri}.ll && cat %{tri}.head ctor2.ll > ctor2-%{tri}.ll && \
# DEFINE:   cat %{tri}.head sctor1.ll > sctor1-%{tri}.ll && cat %{tri}.head sctor2.ll > sctor2-%{tri}.ll && \
# DEFINE:   llc -filetype=obj ctor1-%{tri}.ll -o ctor1-%{tri}.o && llc -filetype=obj ctor2-%{tri}.ll -o ctor2-%{tri}.o && \
# DEFINE:   llc -filetype=obj sctor1-%{tri}.ll -o sctor1-%{tri}.o && llc -filetype=obj sctor2-%{tri}.ll -o sctor2-%{tri}.o && \
# DEFINE:   llvm-as ctor1-%{tri}.ll -o ctor1-%{tri}.bc && llvm-as ctor2-%{tri}.ll -o ctor2-%{tri}.bc && \
# DEFINE:   llvm-as sctor1-%{tri}.ll -o sctor1-%{tri}.bc && llvm-as sctor2-%{tri}.ll -o sctor2-%{tri}.bc && \
# DEFINE:   msil-ld ctor1-%{tri}.o ctor2-%{tri}.o --reference=refs -o o/ctor-%{tri}.exe 2>&1 | count 0 && \
# DEFINE:   msil-ld ctor1-%{tri}.bc ctor2-%{tri}.bc --reference=refs -o lto/ctor-%{tri}.exe 2>&1 | count 0 && \
# DEFINE:   msil-ld sctor1-%{tri}.o sctor2-%{tri}.o --reference=refs -o o/sctor-%{tri}.exe 2>&1 | count 0 && \
# DEFINE:   msil-ld sctor1-%{tri}.bc sctor2-%{tri}.bc --reference=refs -o lto/sctor-%{tri}.exe 2>&1 | count 0 && \
# DEFINE:   llvm-cil-dump o/ctor-%{tri}.exe > ctor-o-%{tri}.txt && \
# DEFINE:   llvm-cil-dump lto/ctor-%{tri}.exe > ctor-lto-%{tri}.txt && \
# DEFINE:   llvm-cil-dump o/sctor-%{tri}.exe > sctor-o-%{tri}.txt && \
# DEFINE:   llvm-cil-dump lto/sctor-%{tri}.exe > sctor-lto-%{tri}.txt && \
# DEFINE:   %{listing} method ctor-o-%{tri}.txt '<Module>' .cctor | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ONCE && \
# DEFINE:   %{listing} method ctor-lto-%{tri}.txt '<Module>' .cctor | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ONCE && \
# DEFINE:   %{listing} method sctor-o-%{tri}.txt '<Module>' .cctor | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PER-UNIT && \
# DEFINE:   %{listing} method sctor-lto-%{tri}.txt '<Module>' .cctor | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PER-UNIT && \
# DEFINE:   FileCheck %s --check-prefix=RESET --input-file=ctor-o-%{tri}.txt && \
# DEFINE:   FileCheck %s --check-prefix=RESET --input-file=ctor-lto-%{tri}.txt
# REDEFINE: %{tri} = win
# RUN: %{ctor}
# REDEFINE: %{tri} = lnx
# RUN: %{ctor}
# REDEFINE: %{tri} = net
# RUN: %{ctor}
## The module initializer, after msil64-unknown-dotnet's guard.
# ONCE:          {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call void() '<Module>'::'__msil_mc_ctor.wreset'{{$}}
# ONCE-NOT:      __msil_mc_ctor
# PER-UNIT:      {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call void() '<Module>'::'__msil_mc_ctor.wsreset{{.*}}'{{$}}
# PER-UNIT-NEXT: {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call void() '<Module>'::'__msil_mc_ctor.wsreset{{.*}}'{{$}}
# PER-UNIT-NOT:  __msil_mc_ctor
# RESET:         {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call void() [FakeWidgets]Contoso.Widget::'Reset'{{$}}

## T-M17's second form.
# RUN: cat lnx.head prio.ll > prio-lnx.ll
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj prio-lnx.ll -o prio-lnx.o
# RUN: llvm-as prio-lnx.ll -o prio-lnx.bc
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj noexp.ll -o noexp.o
# RUN: llvm-as noexp.ll -o noexp.bc
# RUN: not msil-ld ctor1-lnx.bc prio-lnx.bc --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wreset
# RUN: not msil-ld ctor1-lnx.bc prio-lnx.bc -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wreset
# RUN: not msil-ld --lto-emit-asm ctor1-lnx.bc prio-lnx.bc --reference=refs -o x.il 2>&1 \
# RUN:   | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wreset
# RUN: not msil-ld --lto-emit-asm ctor1-lnx.bc prio-lnx.bc -o x.il 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wreset
# RUN: not msil-ld exp1.bc noexp.bc --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld exp1.bc noexp.bc -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld --lto-emit-asm exp1.bc noexp.bc --reference=refs -o x.il 2>&1 \
# RUN:   | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld --lto-emit-asm noexp.bc exp1.bc -o x.il 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld ctor1-lnx.o prio-lnx.o --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wreset
# RUN: not msil-ld prio-lnx.o ctor1-lnx.o --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wreset
# RUN: not msil-ld exp1.o noexp.o --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld noexp.o exp1.o --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld exp1.o noexp.bc --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld noexp.bc exp1.o --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld ctor1-lnx.o prio-lnx.bc --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wreset
# RUN: not msil-ld prio-lnx.bc ctor1-lnx.o --reference=refs -o x.exe 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wreset
# RUN: not msil-ld -shared exp1.o noexp.bc exp2.bc --reference=refs -o x.dll 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
# RUN: not msil-ld -shared noexp.bc exp2.bc exp1.o --reference=refs -o x.dll 2>&1 | %{err} --check-prefix=TM17B -DF=wguard
## ThinLTO modules and LTO partitions.
# RUN: opt -module-summary exp1.ll -o exp1.thin.bc
